    "imageAlt": "Illustrative buyer checklist: compare suppliers on project scope \u2014 not a product photograph.",
    "imageWidth": 2400,
    "imageHeight": 1350,
    "excerpt": "Map bottle, closure, outer packaging and sample-carrier responsibilities before comparing suppliers and coordinated project quotations.",
    "metaDescription": "Source perfume packaging by confirming component responsibilities, bottle fit, sample approval, quotation scope and delivery terms.",
    "body": "*Buyer guidance below separates sourcing tasks from supplier claims. ELAPACK terms are owner-confirmed; actual scope, availability and delivery responsibilities require project confirmation.*\n\nA fragrance project can involve bottles, closures, labels, outer boxes, inserts and sample carriers. This guide helps buyers assign responsibility for those components and compare complete proposals. It does not rank supplier business models or infer manufacturing capability from marketplace listings.\n\n> **Quick answer:** identify who supplies and approves each component, then compare the assembled system. A consolidated supplier may coordinate several components; a specialist may supply only one. Neither model proves manufacturing ownership, lowest cost or fitness for the project. ELAPACK packaging MOQ is 200 pieces; complete sets require 200 of each component.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n\n\n**Map who supplies and approves each component before comparing fragrance-packaging quotations; a consolidated supplier is a coordination choice, not proof of manufacturing ownership or lowest cost.**\n\n\n\n1. Assign responsibility for bottle, closure, outer box, insert, labels, fragrance contact and delivery before requesting quotes.\n\n2. Compare complete quotations \u2014 specifications, minimums, sample scope, packing and landed charges \u2014 not supplier labels.\n\n3. Keep sampling first at every rung: stock samples free plus USD 25 shipping (4\u20137 days); custom samples USD 30 plus USD 25 (3\u20135 days).\n\n4. ELAPACK terms: MOQ 200 pieces per product or component, production typically 15\u201320 days after sample approval and payment receipt.\n\n5. Glass bottles, pumps and sprayers are specified separately through the glass trade and approved on their own samples.\n\n\n\nLast updated November 2026.\n\n## How do you map responsibilities before requesting quotes?\n\n| Component or task | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Bottle and closure | Actual supplier, dimensions, material compatibility and approval sample |\n| Outer box and insert | Finished bottle reference, usable fit and packed assembly |\n| Labels and decoration | Artwork ownership, material compatibility and application responsibility |\n| Filling or fragrance contact | Responsible party and relevant compatibility or regulatory checks |\n| Delivery | Consolidation, packing, freight and acceptance responsibilities |\n\n## Why compare quotations rather than supplier labels?\n\nA marketplace, distributor, coordinator or direct manufacturer may serve different project needs. Request current evidence of the operations relevant to your order and confirm which are subcontracted.\n\n![Illustrative sourcing map: the fragrance-packaging supplier ladder \u2014 marketplace, bundler, print farm, factory-direct \u2014 with glass and print-and-textile trades as separate lanes](/charts/perfume-packaging-supplier-ladder.svg)\n\nCompare specifications, minimums, sample scope, packing and landed charges. Do not assume prototype pricing never improves, that factory-direct always costs less, or that a supplier coordinating glass and print is misleading the buyer.\n\nFor the outer pack, use the [perfume box guide](https://elapack.com/news/wholesale-perfume-boxes-guide/). For fragrance sampling, distinguish blotter cards from vial carriers in the [sample card guide](https://elapack.com/news/perfume-sample-cards-guide/); compatibility and supply responsibilities must be confirmed separately.\n\n## Sample-first, at every rung\n\nThe discipline that transfers across the whole ladder is sample approval before bulk, and its scope should be agreed before comparing cost. At this factory: a stock sample is free with USD 25 courier and typically arrives in 4\u20137 days; a custom sample is USD 30 plus USD 25 shipping, built 3\u20135 days from confirmed artwork. What a sample should prove \u2014 board, print, fit, colour \u2014 is the [packaging samples guide](https://elapack.com/news/custom-packaging-samples-guide/)'s subject, and the questions that separate a factory from a listing are in the [manufacturer vetting checklist](https://elapack.com/news/how-to-vet-a-packaging-manufacturer/). Keep an identifiable approved sample and revision record with the order.\n\n## What does a first branded run look like?\n\n| Line item | Terms |\n|---|---|\n| Boxes, cards, pouches | from this factory, MOQ 200 pieces |\n| Production | typically 15\u201320 days after sample approval and payment receipt; confirm by order |\n| Samples | stock free + USD 25 shipping (delivery 4\u20137 days); custom USD 30 + USD 25 (3\u20135 days) |\n| Glass | from the glass trade \u2014 specified separately, approved on its own samples |\n| Payment and freight | T/T or PayPal; air or sea from Shanghai or Shenzhen |\n\nList the bottle, closure, box, insert and any sample carrier actually needed for the launch. Some programmes need no sample card; confirm each supplier responsibility and schedule separately. The [custom perfume packaging guide](https://elapack.com/news/custom-perfume-packaging-guide/) covers related components. Plan sample review, order-specific production and transit as separate milestones.\n\n## Sources\n\n\n\n1. ELAPACK \u2014 confirmed trade terms (MOQ 200 pieces per product or component; stock samples free with USD 25 shipping, typically 4\u20137 days; custom samples USD 30 plus USD 25 shipping, sample making 3\u20135 days from confirmed artwork; production typically 15\u201320 days after sample approval and payment receipt; payment by T/T or PayPal; air or sea freight from Shanghai or Shenzhen) \u2014 https://elapack.com/\n\n\n\nThis page contains no third-party data. The responsibility-mapping and quotation-comparison questions are buyer guidance; every figure stated comes from ELAPACK's confirmed terms above. Linked ELAPACK guides are cross-references within this site, not external evidence.\n\n## Request a coordinated project quotation\n\nSend the confirmed bottle reference, component list, artwork status, quantities and delivery destination through the [contact page](https://elapack.com/contact/). Ask the quotation to identify supply responsibilities, sample scope and the complete packing arrangement.\n"
